## Proof

Let \(f = u + iv\) be entire, so \(u, v\) are real-valued harmonic functions on \(\mathbb{C}\) satisfying the Cauchy–Riemann equations everywhere:

$$u_x = v_y, \qquad u_y = -v_x.$$

The given condition is the Jacobian determinant of the map \((x,y) \mapsto (u(x,y), v(x,y))\):

$$J = u_x v_y - u_y v_x = 1 \quad \text{on all of } \mathbb{C}.$$

**Step 1: Express the Jacobian via \(f'\).**

Since \(f\) is entire, it is holomorphic everywhere and

$$f'(z) = u_x + i v_x = u_x - i u_y,$$

the last equality using \(v_x = -u_y\). Therefore

$$|f'(z)|^2 = u_x^2 + u_y^2.$$

On the other hand, using the Cauchy–Riemann equations \(v_y = u_x\) and \(v_x = -u_y\),

$$u_x v_y - u_y v_x = u_x \cdot u_x - u_y \cdot (-u_y) = u_x^2 + u_y^2 = |f'(z)|^2.$$

Combining with the hypothesis \(u_x v_y - u_y v_x = 1\), we obtain

$$|f'(z)|^2 = 1 \quad \text{for every } z \in \mathbb{C}. \tag{1}$$

**Step 2: \(f'\) is constant.**

The derivative \(f'\) of an entire function is itself entire. Equation (1) says \(|f'(z)| = 1\) for all \(z \in \mathbb{C}\); that is, \(f'\) is a bounded entire function. By **Liouville's theorem**, \(f'\) is constant.

(Equivalently, \(f'\) is entire and its image lies in the compact set \(S^1\); a nonconstant entire function is an open map by the open mapping theorem, but \(S^1\) has empty interior in \(\mathbb{C}\), a contradiction. Hence \(f'\) is constant.)

So there exists a constant \(a \in \mathbb{C}\) with

$$f'(z) = a \quad \text{for all } z.$$

**Step 3: Determine \(|a|\) and integrate.**

From (1), \(|a|^2 = |f'(z)|^2 = 1\), hence

$$|a| = 1.$$

Integrating \(f'(z) = a\) gives

$$f(z) = az + b$$

for some constant \(b \in \mathbb{C}\).

**Step 4: Converse (sanity check).**

If \(f(z) = az + b\) with \(|a| = 1\), then \(f'(z) = a\) and \(|f'(z)|^2 = |a|^2 = 1\), so the Jacobian \(u_x v_y - u_y v_x = |f'|^2 = 1\) everywhere, confirming the condition is satisfied.

**Conclusion.** The only entire functions satisfying \(u_x v_y - u_y v_x = 1\) on \(\mathbb{C}\) are the affine functions

$$\boxed{f(z) = az + b, \qquad a, b \in \mathbb{C}, \quad |a| = 1.}$$

